Just as an example, I translated `Xoshiro256**` from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xorshift to C#:
```C#
internal class Xoshiro256StarStar
{
    private ulong _s0, _s1, _s2, _s3;

    public unsafe Xoshiro256StarStar()
    {
        do
        {
            Guid g1 = Guid.NewGuid();
            Guid g2 = Guid.NewGuid();
            ulong* g1p = (ulong*)&g1;
            ulong* g2p = (ulong*)&g2;
            _s0 = *g1p;
            _s1 = *(g1p + 1);
            _s2 = *g2p;
            _s3 = *(g2p + 1);
        }
        while ((_s0 | _s1 | _s2 | _s3) == 0);
    }

    private ulong Rol64(ulong x, int k) => (x << k) | (x >> (64 - k));

    public long Next()
    {
        ulong result = Rol64(_s1 * 5, 7) * 9;
        ulong t = _s1 << 17;

        _s2 ^= _s0;
        _s3 ^= _s1;
        _s1 ^= _s2;
        _s0 ^= _s3;

        _s2 ^= t;
        _s3 = Rol64(_s3, 45);

        return (long)result;
    }
}
```
The code is much more concise than the Random code, and it's more than twice as fast:

|         Method |     Mean |     Error |    StdDev |
|--------------- |---------:|----------:|----------:|
| NextXoshiro256 | 3.194 ns | 0.0095 ns | 0.0074 ns |
|   NextRandom64 | 7.644 ns | 0.0916 ns | 0.0857 ns |

while also involving much less allocation for the instance.  It's also reported to have a very good pseudo-random qualities, though I'm admittedly not an expert.

That's all to say, if we're really keen on including a custom generator here, we might as well do it "right".
